Source package: mutter

tap-key.patch
    Lets a modifier tapped on its own send a key combination, configured with tap-key and
    tap-key-sends in org.gnome.mutter. Mutter already gives a lone tap of a modifier a meaning for
    two keys, the overlay key and the locate-pointer key, and this adds a third one that sends
    whatever tap-key-sends names. Held together with other keys it stays an ordinary modifier.

    The combination goes through a virtual keyboard, so it reaches keybindings and global shortcuts
    the same way a key pressed by hand does. configure-gnome-keys.sh has the right Super send
    XF86Launch9, which no physical key sends, for Claude Desktop's Quick Entry shortcut.

    The test is added to src/tests/keybindings.c, which runs during the build. It holds the tap key
    together with another key, which must send nothing, and then taps it on its own, which must
    trigger a keybinding bound to the combination it sends.

    The tap is detected by process_special_modifier_key in src/core/keybindings.c, shared by all
    three keys, and the settings are read in src/core/prefs.c next to the other two. After a version
    bump, check that both still handle the overlay and locate-pointer keys this way, since the patch
    follows them.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login:

      tap the right Super on its own     should send XF86Launch9, which opens Claude Quick Entry
      right Super with another key       should act as Super, for example Ctrl+Super+L locks

xdg-session-management.patch
    Backport of https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/mutter/-/merge_requests/4806 from mutter 51. Mutter
    50 has the session management protocol only under its draft name xx_session_manager_v1, and
    offers it only when gnome-shell runs with MUTTER_DEBUG_SESSION_MANAGEMENT_PROTOCOL=1. The patch
    renames it to the final xdg_session_manager_v1 and offers it to every client. A client which
    names its windows then has mutter restore their workspace, monitor, position, size and state.

    It is for Firefox, which binds only xdg_session_manager_v1 since version 154. Firefox restores
    its windows with it only together with the patches in patches/firefox/, which fix Mozilla bug
    2064100 and enable the protocol on GNOME again, and the one in patches/gtk+3.0/, which adds the
    xdg-toplevel-realized signal Firefox needs. Without them, MOZ_LOG=WidgetWayland:4 shows which
    check in IsSessionRestoreSupported (Firefox's widget/gtk/nsAppShell.cpp) disabled it.

    Firefox sends get_session at every start, even where it does not register its windows. Mutter
    50.1 and 51.1 crash when a client sends get_session after the session manager could not be
    created, which happens when ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/session.gvdb cannot be read (mutter
    issue 5168, open). This patch makes that crash reachable. If gnome-shell ever crashes when
    Firefox starts, look in the journal for "Could not create session manager" and move that file
    away.

    session.gvdb only grows, by about 270 bytes for every window Firefox ever had. Firefox never
    removes a window from its session, and mutter never prunes stored windows on its own. Mutter
    also cannot remove a window stored in an earlier run of a session, so a client could not clean
    up even if it tried (mutter issue 5174, open, with patches). Moving the file away resets all
    stored window places, of every application.

    The patch is the four commits of the merge request. Only the test change of the last one
    differs: upstream it edits src/tests/wayland-xdg-session-management-tests.c, which was split
    out of src/tests/wayland-unit-tests.c after 50.1, so here the same line is removed from
    wayland-unit-tests.c. The existing session management tests in wayland-unit-tests.c run during
    the build against the renamed protocol.

    Drop the patch once Ubuntu ships mutter 51 or newer. Before that, after a version bump, check
    that src/meson.build still has no xdg-session-management entry of its own, and note that with
    wayland-protocols 1.48 or newer the build uses the protocol XML from wayland-protocols instead of
    the copy in src/wayland/protocol/.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login, with wayland-info from the wayland-utils package:

      wayland-info | grep session        should list xdg_session_manager_v1 and no xx_session_manager_v1
